us  they are a whole lot smaller than us so  that's where we are going to focus today  what happens when one tiny creature  decides to live on another tiny creature  there's a name for this kind of  relationship epibiosis and it's not  limited to the microcosmos or to water  environments it might help to think of  it as the flip side of another  relationship we've watched play out in  the micro Cosmos  endosymbiosis where one creature decides  to live on the inside of another one  like these algae growing inside of a  Hydra but the relationship between  endosymbionts and their host can look  different depending on the species but  in cases like these it might be the host  providing shelter in the form of their  bodies in exchange for nutrients made by  their endosymbiont in contrast epibiosis  is when one creature decides to live on  the outside of another one and sometimes  epibiosis is super obvious like it was  with the copepod that we started out  with but take a look at this dynamoeba  this organism was first described by the  American naturalist Joseph Lighty in the  19th century and from a distance it  doesn't seem like the dynamoeba has much  going on there's certainly no ornate  flowery growths protruding from it but  when you zoom in you can see all sorts  of bacteria and archaea arranged on its  surface in this case those bacteria and  Archaea are epibions which is the name  that we give to organisms growing on top  of another organism and the dynamoeba is  the base of ion the organism that the  epibience are growing upon and in the  microcosmos sometimes you are the basal  biont and other times you are the  epibiont this leafy ciliate zuthamnium  for example can be found growing on  copepods similar to how we found our  ciliates in the beginning of this video  but we've also found zuthamnium with  euglenoids growing on them like their  green sprinkles that stay glued to their  stalks and we've even seen strange sites  like these prokaryotes growing on top of  a larger prokaryote although we are not  sure what species they are or what they  might be getting out of this nearness to  one another and given the diversity in  epibience the way that they attach  themselves to the base of ion varies a  lot as well  for the smallest microbes their path  towards their new Surface is shaped by  the viscous forces around them that  creates a wall of water they have to  break through before they can latch onto  their new home and for unicellular  eukaryotes or even multicellular ones  the decision to attach itself to another  creature might just be another part of a  longer life cycle a shift from a free  swimming self that ages into something  willing to settle down using an adhesive  molecule to glue them together and if  you are the basa biont welcoming these  epibience might actually work out well  for you these new residents might  provide you with some nutrition that you  are lacking supplying metabolic  byproducts that are now in very close  proximity these epibions might even help  you hide from predators masking the  chemical cues that give your location  away but there are trade-offs as well  for one you are literally shouldering  the weight of other organisms in one  water that can make all the difference  affecting your speed and buoyancy and  epibions can even get in the way of  important needs blocking off sun or  impeding reproductive organs from their  usual functions meanwhile for the  epibayant there are pros and cons as  well life on another creature means that  you've got free Transportation wherever  your vasobiont chooses to go and these  surfaces often make for great places to  grab the remains of whatever your  bazobiont is eating but life is also  unstable on a living surface when your  basal biont grows or sheds or  experiences some other major life change  you the epibiont will find the very  ground beneath you changing as well  there's this disadvantage inherent to  both the epibiant and basal biond life  in one paper we found the term they used  was shared Doom which is a more poetic  way to say this if they get eaten you  get eaten too and it is a strange  consequence to think about that this  partnership makes its individuals  vulnerable to new threats the basabiant  now faces the epibions Predators and  vice versa as their identities have  co-mingled and blurred together and yet  they are still two distinct entities one  lived upon the other living upon so ask  the question again which would you  rather be the epibiont or the basaviant  or maybe there is little difference  between the two each shaped by the whims  of the other because ultimately in some  way every organism on Earth it has to be  said is both lived upon and lives upon  just as we are shaped by the microbes on  our skin and the Earth under our feet  but next week we're gonna focus on one  case of ebibiosis where we think the  answer is perhaps a bit cleaner  thank you for coming on this journey  with us as we explore the Unseen World  that surrounds us and we'd also like to  say thank you to skillshare for  supporting this video you can learn a  lot on skillshare from gardening to  cooking or illustration you can learn  how to script shoot and edit your own  YouTube videos from one of the most  successful creators on the platform  Marquez Brownlee drawing on a decade of  experience Marquez opens up his process  like never before guiding you step by  step through every stage of content  creation from scripting and  storyboarding to shooting and editing  you'll learn how to create videos that  connect whether you have one follower or  one hundred thousand skillshare is an  online learning community with thousands  of inspiring classes for anyone who  loves learning and wants to explore  their creativity and learning skills  with so much to explore and real world  projects to create skillshare empowers  you to accomplish real growth it's  curated specifically for learning  meaning there are no ads to distract you  they're always launching new premium  classes so you can stay focused on  leveling up your skills if you're one of  the first thousand people to click on  the link in the description you can get  a one month free trial of skillshare's  Premium membership  and speaking of organisms relying on  other organisms these are our patreon  patrons they are the people who make it  possible to make the thing that we make  thank you so much to all of them for  being a part of what we do it is a  pretty small percentage of the folks who  watch who make it possible for everyone  to enjoy what we do here at Journey to  the micro Cosmos so if you want to join  me in feeling grateful to them right now  I very much appreciate it and if you  would like to join them you can find us  at patreon.com journey to micro if you  want to see more from our master of  microscopes James Weiss you can check  out jam and germs on Instagram and if  you want to see more from us there's  always a subscribe button somewhere  nearby  foreign
 

